rationale:
The three tests in `tests/authoring_cli.rs` that carry `{test}` references to `requirements.cli.behavior_review_guidance` adequately check its obligations. They are `bundled_skills_direct_behavior_review_against_requirements`, `bundled_skills_mark_roots_and_handle_uncovered_items` and `bundled_skills_direct_the_reference_procedure_and_report_items_without_one`. The requirement governs the text of the bundled skills, so I judged the tests as phrase-presence checks on that text. I did not need implementation source.

The helpers `assert_phrases`, `assert_text` and `assert_text_in_order` really assert. `assert_text` panics unless the text contains each phrase. `assert_text_in_order` also enforces order. `section` panics if a heading is missing and bounds the text to that section. `assert_phrases` runs over the whole bundled skill, so the checks bind to real skill content.

How each obligation maps to a test:

- **Inspect every end-user-observable result and branch, including errors and input handling, and verify no unagreed behavior.** `bundled_skills_direct_behavior_review_against_requirements` asserts:
  - the `habeas-requirements` phrase "Every end-user-observable result of the code, including errors and input handling, must follow from a requirement the user agreed to";
  - the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` phrases "distinct observable branches" and "every observable branch has been reviewed";
  - the `habeas-evidence` phrase "Behavior with no requirement needs one agreed with the user".
- **Verify the code fulfills each applicable requirement.** The same test asserts "The supervising agent must still check that the code fulfills each applicable requirement" in the "What the review checks" section.
- **Explain the review criteria, including test adequacy and justified absence of tests.** The same test asserts the following in the "What the review checks" section:
  - wrong expected results;
  - an unmarked standalone test (a marking gap);
  - untested obligations, boundaries and failure cases;
  - the omission exemptions (no logic, network, real time, process-wide state);
  - the rule that "stays unchanged, distinct, or present" always needs a test;
  - the criteria for examples;
  - "One acknowledged gap does not excuse others".

  It also asserts that a `false` rationale names a listed gap.
- **Explain the three outcomes and the documented-gap convention.** The same test asserts the three outcome phrases, the FIXME object form, `// :github:`, "A plain `FIXME` or `TODO` comment does not count", and "To defer a requirement gap the user agrees to, use `{fixme}`".
- **Mark roots with `{impl}`, tests with `{test}`, leave reached code unmarked, and use `{cite}` only for related code.** Three tests cover this:
  - The behavior test asserts the ordered procedure steps and "Leave what it reaches unmarked".
  - `bundled_skills_mark_roots_and_handle_uncovered_items` asserts the root definition and the downward-only reach, and checks that other skills refer to the root definition rather than restate it.
  - `bundled_skills_direct_the_reference_procedure_and_report_items_without_one` asserts the ordered procedure and that other skills defer to it.
- **Handle no-recent-coverage warnings and decide dead code before changing anything.** `bundled_skills_mark_roots_and_handle_uncovered_items` asserts that the "Uncovered Rust items" section tells the agent to:
  - choose references by "Choosing references";
  - determine "whether it is a requirement root, an entry point, an item that serves other code, or dead code" before changing the item;
  - report dead code without editing it.

  The reference-procedure test also asserts the dead-code reporting text.
- **Interacting requirements, and a citation alone is not an explanation.** The behavior test asserts that the skill says to mark each requirement with `{impl}` where it applies and add fenced commentary on how they combine. It also asserts "a reference alone would not say why `^C` is accepted" and the bootstrap-skill counterpart.

Unresolved question: the requirement asks the skills to explain the criteria in `requirements.ci.review_criteria` generally. The tests do not assert every sub-criterion, such as ambiguity treated as a gap, counterexample requirements, or delegation to refining requirements and site reviews. The requirement names test adequacy and justified absence of tests explicitly, and both are asserted. I treat the unasserted sub-criteria as unnamed edge cases rather than gaps.

I found no marking gap, no wrong expected result and no weak assertion. The tests are marked `{test}` for the subject.
